// Expected: a1 at one session and at two.
//
// Bob opens c with an unchecked decryption and then splits the plaintext into
// two fields under a check. The attacker sends nil: Bob's decryption runs on
// it, which is a use of c, and the split fails as the last step that
// mentions c. Under positional acceptance this is a forgery, as it is when
// the failing check binds one output (auth_accept_then_halt.vp).
//
// Each output of an assignment is its own step, and the second output of the
// halted split was once read as a later use of c that the run never reached,
// so it cancelled the acceptance: a0 at one session, where the same model
// with `a = SPLIT(d)?` gave a1. Every output of the halted assignment is now
// the failed use. auth_accept_then_split_halt_later_use.vp adds a use after
// the split, which the halt does leave unreached.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	generates m1, m2
	c = ENC(k, CONCAT(m1, m2))
]

Alice -> Bob: c

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	d = DEC(k, c)
	a, b = SPLIT(d)?
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: c
]
